To determine how many square centimeters of paper Jackson could cover with paint, we need to calculate the area of the strip he can make. The area of a rectangle is given by the formula:

\[ \text{Area} = \text{length} \times \text{width} \]

First, we need to convert all measurements to the same unit. Since the final answer is required in square centimeters, we will convert the length from meters to centimeters and the width from millimeters to centimeters.

1 meter = 100 centimeters
1 millimeter = 0.1 centimeters

So, the length of the strip in centimeters is:
\[ 25 \text{ meters} = 25 \times 100 = 2500 \text{ centimeters} \]

And the width of the strip in centimeters is:
\[ 6.5 \text{ millimeters} = 6.5 \times 0.1 = 0.65 \text{ centimeters} \]

Now, we can calculate the area of the strip:
\[ \text{Area} = 2500 \text{ cm} \times 0.65 \text{ cm} = 1625 \text{ square centimeters} \]

Therefore, the number of square centimeters of paper that Jackson could cover with paint is:
\[ \boxed{1625} \]

